{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And he called upon the name of HaShem.</b> Onkelos explains (Onkelos on Genesis 12:8) that he prayed there, as in \"I called upon Your name, HaShem, from the lowest pit\" (Lamentations 3:55). But the correct interpretation is that he would call out in a loud voice there before the altar, proclaiming the name of HaShem, making Him and His divinity known to the children of men. For in Ur of the Chaldeans he had been teaching them, but they refused to listen. And now, when he came to this land concerning which he had been promised, \"I will bless those who bless you\" (Genesis 12:3), he was accustomed to teach and to publicize the divinity. And similarly the verse states concerning Isaac (Genesis 26:24–25), when he went to the valley of Gerar and was promised, \"Fear not, for I am with you,\" that he built an altar and \"called upon the name of HaShem\" — for he had come to a new place where they had not heard of Him and had not seen His glory, and he declared His glory among those nations. And the same is not said of Jacob, because he begot many sons, all of them servants of HaShem, and he had a great assembly called the congregation of Israel, and the faith was publicized among them and made known to every people. And moreover, since the days of his fathers it had been publicized throughout all the land of Canaan. And thus they said in Bereshit Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 39:16): this teaches that he caused the name of the Holy One, blessed be He, to be spoken in the mouth of every creature."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:27:42.539106014Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}